#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "6a082146-0d63-59dd-9224-a319d73ca59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4827" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](images/ac/acda5be1511b340b3a947cbaa4dedfd7a3175029d2937481b261c7a0c4eb5348.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -\\mathbf{b}_2$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = -3\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](images/ac/ac75a1bca2b8012016657f4d1d88fdf1725c84b2a3ec48376bbcb4670f134ba3.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =2(-{b}_{2})-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/d9/d98192a7e16e605311ee2bb8791e6d86005c6d565ede80e68aaaeaf8097721a2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "f07356b8-aeb3-50ac-b017-c370d87395d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4828" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and ![](images/1e/1e4b0e4345ef6d0bd0b350cc973eaa7c15b654e76b06cd6f42740af0ab8fc877.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](images/a5/a5e125038beb90ff4fd2b16bea5b08f83855c80f2a70a73ccfc15fa53fd45a30.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2,$ and $\\mathbf c_3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3{b}_{2})-5(-{b}_{3})+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/f7/f78654ee0612c6daa7e7a1fa26fb56ce0c3c94f6a7ba29805a569eac643946eb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "91fac4cd-7097-5244-a385-9f3f76395912" :question/math-academy-id "e-4829" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](images/ae/aed9216ce4c536aad07f26eec0e0cb7e3032147a19c13e0a2d42925083c49518.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\mathbf{b}_2-\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = 2\\mathbf{b}_1 - 3\\mathbf{b}_2?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $\\mathbf{b}$'s in terms of $\\mathbf{c}$'s. Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2 = -\\mathbf{b}_2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\left( -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{b}_1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_1 = -3\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSince ![](images/80/80531bde3f2ebb6314a3a297efa44e3e21a33d0b5f628c67c39ef0fa288f4c78.png) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})-3(-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+6{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/f6/f678c1ecc9878c30b04e891af9e6933f1edb42af8e155c3f4719838210e1cc1d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02827c4d-78d8-5edb-b4b1-a66c8c3572ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380624} {:answer/id #uuid "07ba18a1-c573-5b12-af3e-f63e9a02c6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380625} {:answer/id #uuid "e4316839-856c-58f8-a3bd-6651a6284c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380626} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9700a5-8075-5cc2-ab61-d44802827f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380627} {:answer/id #uuid "be31030c-d727-58ad-89f7-7c11aefdedb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02827c4d-78d8-5edb-b4b1-a66c8c3572ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380624} :answer-field/id #uuid "021b66aa-8b26-50b0-990d-6ef58bb0ea70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e990e0e8-cf10-53f1-8f77-276602bc53e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-112406" :question/problem "Let $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where ${b}_{1}={c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\  & =2{c}_{2}-{c}_{1} \\\\  & =2{c}_{2}+(-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43596b54-0af5-56a0-a954-2be5f40712fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244900} {:answer/id #uuid "8b479d14-ade5-5b29-9efe-2745148ba46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245536} {:answer/id #uuid "be780025-c235-58a6-8299-86b26eaeee98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246012} {:answer/id #uuid "e089e494-fe55-562d-9268-21868311ca66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246310} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c12ab0-ecc5-5590-8676-6569049bf30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b479d14-ade5-5b29-9efe-2745148ba46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245536} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d089c3-601c-50be-8dc4-891bcf11d265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5c48c40-2fd0-5515-867f-1f3be83f3dfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-112656" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}+8{b}_{3} \\\\  & =8{b}_{3}+2{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(4{b}_{3})+2{b}_{1}+2\\cdot 2(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+4{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c5508b-548a-5140-8c96-c97300fbfa53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317714} {:answer/id #uuid "4ffee88d-bdfe-542f-8981-b89c64f6f164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317829} {:answer/id #uuid "67a1d297-17f9-5b1f-94c8-0903c160b639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317920} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f18c83-77c2-5633-9815-02a5d99ad08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318276} {:answer/id #uuid "fb880ea1-b066-5d18-86b6-7e9e19bedb73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ffee88d-bdfe-542f-8981-b89c64f6f164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317829} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e009c0-a4a5-5b04-a0aa-ec831ac7ff71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "336edbee-8e37-5f76-9b04-8490bfd6d48b" :question/math-academy-id "q-34769" :question/problem "Let $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where ${b}_{1}=-{c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=3{c}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{c}_{2}+{c}_{1} \\\\  & =2(-{c}_{2})+\\frac{1}{3}(3{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{b}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024c800d-e686-5099-aac6-b0e31f54afd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317579} {:answer/id #uuid "15f11330-47e6-5ed5-9561-96bd29d13350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317634} {:answer/id #uuid "6d552c22-6987-5fa6-953a-bb2a1c040c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317942} {:answer/id #uuid "99824331-e524-54e4-b871-2d1d5411d488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318090} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0284d0-70cb-54a6-852c-36dda2394925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99824331-e524-54e4-b871-2d1d5411d488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318090} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa250df4-57b1-5a91-99d7-1cbcff4b1807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8db838b1-f0b2-5003-b1ee-a4f1dfa03764" :question/math-academy-id "q-34770" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}={b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=-3{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-5{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3}+4{b}_{1} \\\\  & =5(-{b}_{2})+2{b}_{3}-4(-{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-2{b}_{2})+2{c}_{2}-\\frac{4}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point