\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=-{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}-{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{b}_{1}+5{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3(-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})+5(-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}-5{c}_{1} \\\\  & =-8{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9e7207-f73d-53ee-8eea-c28fc296a8ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382429} {:answer/id #uuid "9a35966c-bc96-5e1e-a448-8501426b955b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382430} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6ee5d8-68ee-59a3-b947-9b6be042f3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382431} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e67ff0-33de-5988-9be8-65a3853d47a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382432} {:answer/id #uuid "bf081b28-a050-5ec7-95f3-c7be3546bdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a35966c-bc96-5e1e-a448-8501426b955b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382430} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb8cf9da-96ae-5d88-83d0-d8588cf154a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99687278-d62b-5cdc-9583-90e343f4b850" :question/math-academy-id "q-81227" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=2{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}+7{b}_{3} \\\\  & =7{b}_{3}+4{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-7(-{b}_{3})+8(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1})-(2{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a1b575e-fa01-51f5-b88d-283c9336ee0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382436} {:answer/id #uuid "5757bcb0-1793-5c4b-a36d-da0ddc690d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382437} {:answer/id #uuid "2db15541-a9d2-5290-a335-80a3f8f7b7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382438} {:answer/id #uuid "4122fd9a-e1eb-5881-8e54-90119cdba595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382439} {:answer/id #uuid "48831eba-196d-5db6-a3d4-3b4736fdb543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4122fd9a-e1eb-5881-8e54-90119cdba595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382439} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ae9640-12bf-561a-992d-513ae104cb9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c8f8f3b4-bc3b-5876-ac7a-65d4abd5765a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81231" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}+4{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2})+4(\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{16}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
